.TH CYAML_NODE_SET_TAG 3 "2026-08-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_node_set_tag \- attach, replace, or clear a node's YAML tag
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cyaml.h>

ccol_retval_t cyaml_node_set_tag(cyaml node, const char *tag);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Attach or replace node's tag, or clear it.  An internal copy of tag
is made.

This sets metadata only; it never coerces node's existing type or
value.  Setting "tag:yaml.org,2002:str" on an already-constructed
CYAML_INTEGER node leaves it a CYAML_INTEGER still carrying that
(now type-inconsistent) tag string; a caller wanting a node
genuinely typed as a string should construct one
(cyaml_create_string(3)) and tag it, not tag an existing node of a
different type.

A tag set here is preserved across a later cyaml_set() call on the
same node, which mutates only the node's value.  A tag that does not
match the node's own type is also preserved across
cyaml_serialize()/cyaml_serialize_flow(): it is emitted in its
fully-resolved verbatim form exactly like a custom tag, rather than
being silently dropped as a matching core-schema tag would be (see
cyaml_serialize(3)).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
node  Target node of any type; a no-op if NULL.
tag   New tag string (copied); NULL clears any existing tag.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success, ccol_invalid_args if node is NULL, or
ccol_not_enough_memory on allocation failure (node's existing tag,
if any, is left untouched in that case).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_node_tag (3),
.BR cyaml_set (3),
.BR cyaml_create_string (3)
